  <section>
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>Camden is one of Maine's most iconic coastal towns — Camden Hills, the harbor, the Opera House, Penobscot Bay sailing. But despite heavy tourism and a vibrant year-round community, <strong>Camden has no cannabis dispensaries</strong>. Visitors looking for legal cannabis need to drive 5-7 minutes to Northport or Rockland.</p>
    <p>This is a notable gap. Camden's affluent, tourism-heavy customer base is exactly the demographic that premium cannabis retailers target. The absence of local dispensaries means Camden's cannabis revenue flows to neighboring towns.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Nearest Dispensaries to Camden</h2>

    <h3>Bayside Bud Shack (Northport — between Belfast &amp; Camden)</h3>
    <p><strong>Address:</strong> 1369 Atlantic Hwy, Northport, ME 04849<br>
    <strong>Phone:</strong> (207) 505-7223<br>
    <strong>Distance from Camden:</strong> ~5 mi north<br>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Medical + Recreational<br>
    <strong>Rating:</strong> 4.9/5</p>
    <p>Bayside Bud Shack is Camden's nearest option in either direction for both medical and recreational. Their MOFGA-certified organic flower, solventless concentrates, and additive-free vape cartridges appeal to Camden's quality-conscious demographic. They offer 5% veteran discount, 3% rewards, ADA access, and curbside pickup.</p>

    <h3>Rockland Options (~7 mi south)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Highbrow</strong> — Recreational (696 Main St, Rockland)</li>
      <li><strong>Scrimshaw Cannabis</strong> — Medical (500 Main St, Rockland, 207-466-9408)</li>
      <li><strong>Cannabis Cured</strong> — Medical/Recreational (Thomaston area, Route 1)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Camden Gap</h2>
    <p>For a town with Camden's profile, the absence of a dispensary is striking. The reasons are typical: Camden's town government has not permitted cannabis retail. Given the town's reputation as Maine's "crown jewel" coastal destination, there is economic potential for a dispensary that matches the community's aesthetic — premium, boutique, quality-focused. Whether this gap closes depends on whether Camden follows the pattern of other opted-in coastal towns.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why Camden</h2>
    <p>Camden is one of Maine's most recognizable place names — and for good reason. The harbor (perennially rated among the east coast's most photogenic by travel outlets), Camden Hills State Park with its views from Mount Battie and Mount Megunticook, the Camden Opera House drawing national touring acts, and the Penobscot Bay sailing scene combine into a single tourism brand that draws shoulder-season and off-season visitors alongside the summer peak. About 5,500 people live here year-round, but the Census Bureau classifies roughly one in ten housing units as "seasonal," and the town's lodging-tax receipts confirm that the population effectively doubles or triples between Memorial Day and Columbus Day.</p>
    <p>The customer base for a hypothetical Camden dispensary would be one of the most attractive in Maine. Census ACS data puts Camden's median household income comfortably above the state average — Knox County's wealthiest town by most income measures — and the demographic skew runs older (retirees, second-home owners from Boston / New York / Connecticut, summer sailing households) and well-educated. This is exactly the demographic premium retailers target: customers willing to pay for craft flower, solventless concentrates, and well-staffed budtender consultations rather than chasing promotional pricing.</p>
    <p>Geographically, Camden sits on US-1 between Rockland (about 12 minutes south) and Belfast (about 20 minutes north). Most Midcoast tourists pass through Camden on the way to or from Bar Harbor, Acadia, or the Blue Hill peninsula. For a visitor who wants quality cannabis and is already driving this corridor, both Bayside Bud Shack (Northport, ~5 mi north on US-1) and the Rockland Main Street dispensary cluster (~7 mi south) are practical stops — but the absence of an in-town option makes Camden a case study in how local municipal politics, not market logic, shapes Maine's cannabis retail map.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Visiting Nearest Camden Dispensaries</h2>
    <p><strong>Driving in from Camden.</strong> US-1 north (toward Belfast): turn left at the Northport light onto Atlantic Hwy; Bayside Bud Shack is at 1369 Atlantic Hwy, ~10 minutes from Camden center. US-1 south (toward Rockland): the drive is ~12 minutes; Rockland dispensaries (Highbrow at 696 Main, Scrimshaw at 500 Main) are walkable from public parking on Main Street. Both routes are signed and well-paved year-round; winter travel adds a few minutes but does not require anything beyond a normal vehicle.</p>
    <p><strong>Payment.</strong> Maine dispensary norm is cash or debit-card processed through Hypur or VTPC. Bayside Bud Shack and all three Rockland stores run on-site ATMs. Credit cards are not accepted at any Maine adult-use dispensary by state regulation.</p>
    <p><strong>What to bring.</strong> A valid U.S. state photo ID for adult-use purchases. Medical patients should bring a Maine medical card (or a valid card from a reciprocity state — Maine accepts cards from all neighboring states; cross-check the OCP reciprocity list if you are out of state).</p>
    <p><strong>Hours.</strong> Bayside Bud Shack closes 7 pm most days. Highbrow in Rockland runs later on some weeknights (sometimes to 8 pm). Scrimshaw Cannabis (medical only) closes 6 pm. Plan to arrive by 6 pm if Scrimshaw is your target stop.</p>
    <p><strong>Accessibility.</strong> Bayside Bud Shack is explicitly listed as ADA-accessible in their materials; curbside pickup is available. Rockland's Main Street dispensaries are all single-floor retail and ADA-accessible. None of the Camden-area options currently offer home delivery — the closest delivery coverage is Belfast south and Portland south.</p>
  </section>


